  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming and drying trend expected through the weekend.\n\n-  Cold front Monday-Tuesday may usher in cooler, breezy and \n   unsettled weather for much of next week.\n\nAfter low clouds, patchy fog and lingering showers dissipate by \nlater this morning, temperatures will rise into the 50s across\nsouthwest Montana(~10 degrees below average in Butte), 60s across\nwestern Montana and close to 80 degrees in the lower valleys of \nnorth- central Idaho. \n\nTemperatures will be summer-like by the weekend. If you plan on \nrecreating near water during this time, be aware that water \ntemperatures are quite cold. Plunging into cold water can become \ndangerous if you aren't prepared for it. Cold shock can cause \ndramatic changes in breathing, heart rate and blood pressure...and\ncan be life-threatening for anyone without protection from the \ntemperatures or a lifejacket to help you stay afloat. For more \ninformation go to weather.gov/safety/coldwater\n\nOn Friday, moisture embedded in northwest flow could trigger some\nshower activity across extreme northwest Montana and in Glacier \nNational Park. There is enough instability that an isolated \nthunderstorm can't be ruled out. \n\nBy early next week a trough of low pressure will bring unsettled\nweather to the Pacific Northwest and eventually into central Idaho\nand western Montana. There is large spread in the weather models\non the finer details. When looking at the ensemble clusters for \nMonday, 61 percent of the models have a slower/milder solution \nwhile the remaining 39 percent have a faster/cooler/wetter \nsolution. This trough will cut off and meander, bringing showery \nweather all the way through mid-week. If you have any outdoor \nplans during the week, you may want to bring rain gear just in \ncase the wetter solutions materialize.",
